To generate the assert statement for the given comment, we need to express the condition that for every valid index from 0 to the old size minus one, if the element at that index in the list is null, then the next element in the old list was also null; otherwise, the element at that index is equal to the next element in the old list.

Here's how we can translate this into an assert statement using the provided rules:

1. We need to compare the elements of the list before and after the removal of the first element.
2. We need to check each index from 0 to the old size minus one.
3. We need to use the `\old` syntax to refer to the state of the list before the method execution.
4. We need to use the `get` method to access the elements of the list.

The assert statement can be written as follows:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size())-1; (\old(this.get(i)) == null) => (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null) || this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i+1));</code>

This assert statement checks that for each valid index `i` in the range from 0 to the old size minus two, if the element at index `i` in the old list is null, then the element at index `i+1` in the old list must also be null. Otherwise, the element at index `i` in the current list must be equal to the element at index `i+1` in the old list.
